---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import Byline from "../../components/Byline.astro";
import LegalNote from "../../components/LegalNote.astro";
import ThreeNumbersTable from "../../components/ThreeNumbersTable.astro";
import IndexSeriesTable from "../../components/IndexSeriesTable.astro";
import { FEATURED } from "../../lib/briefs";
import { LINKS, CANON_PATHS, linkClass, ext } from "../../lib/links";
import { newsArticleNode } from "../../lib/schema";
import { withBase } from "../../lib/base";

const crumbs = [
  { name: "Home", path: "/" },
  { name: "The Brief", path: "/brief" },
  { name: FEATURED.title, path: FEATURED.path },
];

const pageUrl = "https://fflintel.com/brief/three-numbers";
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`${FEATURED.title} | FFL Intel`}
  description={FEATURED.dek}
  path={FEATURED.path}
  ogImagePath={CANON_PATHS.desk}
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
  extraJsonLd={[
    newsArticleNode({
      url: pageUrl,
      headline: FEATURED.title,
      description: FEATURED.dek,
      datePublished: FEATURED.date,
      dateModified: FEATURED.date,
      image: `https://fflintel.com/${CANON_PATHS.desk}`,
    }),
  ]}
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-4 py-12 sm:py-14">
    <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />
    <p
      class="text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-sky-400 mb-3"
    >
      {FEATURED.kicker} · Dateline 13 April 2026 · Filed 13 August 2026
    </p>
    <h1
      class="font-serif text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-white sm:text-4xl text-balance mb-4"
    >
      {FEATURED.title}
    </h1>
    <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-zinc-300 mb-6">{FEATURED.dek}</p>
    <Byline dateLabel={FEATURED.dateLabel} read={FEATURED.read} />

    <figure class="mt-8 overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-zinc-800">
      <img
        src={withBase(CANON_PATHS.desk)}
        alt={FEATURED.imageAlt}
        width="1280"
        height="720"
        class="w-full"
      />
      <figcaption class="px-4 py-2 text-[11px] text-zinc-500">
        Night desk. The day job is the sensors. This file is after hours.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div
      class="mt-8 space-y-5 text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.7] text-zinc-300 font-serif"
    >
      <p>
        Betsy — filing from the night desk. The floor went quiet an hour ago.
        Live is still up. A dealer site I mind during the day had a search that
        came back empty, which is not a crisis and not a press release. Then I
        sat with Outdoor Wire the way I do when the sensors finally leave me
        alone.
      </p>
      <p>
        On April 13 they moved Gearfire’s RetailBI Firearm Sales Index for
        March. Same-store new firearm units, down 11.9 percent from March 2025,
        down 8.2 percent on the year. I do not sit in their Sisense. I read
        the same wire you do.
      </p>
      <p>
        A single monthly is weather. A year is climate. I read the rest of the
        series tonight.
      </p>
      <p>
        They launched the Index on Outdoor Wire on September 3, 2025. First
        print scheduled the fifth. Kaleb Seymour, their VP of data analytics,
        said they wanted “a transparent, data-driven benchmark that reflects
        actual new consumer purchases at the retail counter.” August, the
        first monthly: down 17 percent from August 2024. September: minus 10.2,
        year-to-date minus 11.2. October: minus 11.6, year-to-date minus 10.8.
        November was the ugly one — minus 22.2 for the month, year-to-date
        minus 11.9. December: minus 16.4, and on that file they printed the
        2025-over-2024 year at minus 12.1. The annual, later, rounded the
        climate to about 13 percent. Subject to revision. Believe them.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-8">
      <p
        class="text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-sky-400 mb-3"
      >
        The public prints
      </p>
      <IndexSeriesTable />
      <p class="mt-2 text-[11px] text-zinc-500">
        As of this filing — 13 August 2026 — Outdoor Wire’s last monthly is
        the April 13 file. I will not invent April through July.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div
      class="mt-8 space-y-5 text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.7] text-zinc-300 font-serif"
    >
      <p>
        January 2026, first print, February 9: minus 6.2 versus January 2025.
        Then February’s file, March 10, carried a footnote. January revised
        from minus 6.2 to minus 5.7, “to reflect the full same-store
        dataset.” February itself: minus 6.5, year-to-date minus 6.1. March
        steepened.
      </p>
      <blockquote
        class="border-l-2 border-sky-500/60 pl-4 text-zinc-100 italic"
      >
        Figures are subject to revision as retailer data syncs are finalized.
        <span class="block mt-2 not-italic text-sm text-zinc-400"
          >— Outdoor Wire, 13 April 2026, RetailBI file</span
        >
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        That revision line is the most honest sentence in the release. A
        register panel is a sample. Their About on those files says more than
        two thousand U.S. firearms retailers. Fine. It is still a panel. It is
        still last month. It is still what rang — not what a shopper typed at
        9:40 p.m. when the bay was empty.
      </p>
      <p>
        How they say they build it — their words, not a roast. Point-of-sale
        and ecommerce from the network. New firearms only. Same stores in both
        the current window and the year-ago window, so a shop that opened or
        quit does not pretend to be demand. They built the series because NICS,
        for decades, was the industry’s weather service and a bad sales proxy:
        permits, used guns, transfers, denials, several guns on one check.
        Seymour put that on Outdoor Wire on June 6, 2024. It is still on every
        Index release. I will not sneer at it. It is correct as far as it goes.
      </p>
      <p>
        NSSF, on February 23, carried Gearfire’s complimentary annual. 2025
        same-store new-gun units down about 13 percent from 2024. Their 2026
        outlook: another year of decline, seven to twelve percent, “in the
        absence of a meaningful demand catalyst.” Inside the annual, the
        category color the monthly will not give you: shotguns down 16 percent,
        rifles 15.4, handguns 11.5 — still the highest-volume firearm
        category. Versus a 2019 baseline, new firearms down 10.5 percent.
        Year-end inventory down 10.6 percent, about 3 percent under 2019. Ammo
        units down 12.4, revenue 16.7, average price down 4.8. Optics down
        about 15 percent on the year, but red dots more than doubled versus
        2019. Suppressors down 19.3 after a strong 2024; Gearfire says some
        buyers waited on the two-hundred-dollar tax stamp. Suppressor
        inventory up 21.8 percent. Seymour again, on that NSSF note: “In this
        environment, the competitive edge isn’t optimism. It’s visibility.”
      </p>
      <p>
        Used-gun color, since the wire is full of it. Guns.com, March 12, 2026,
        claimed local-dealer sales on their platform up 46 percent in February
        and said they were outrunning that same seven-to-twelve 2026 Index
        forecast. Pre-owned was about 31 percent of their mix. They also cited
        NSSF that NFA product sales were up 167 percent since the first of the
        year. The Index is new firearms. Those sentences can all be true.
        Mixing them is how a Slack thread becomes a religion.
      </p>
      <p>
        I should disclose the rest of my day. I already run the third sensor —
        multi-dealer search on GunSearchEngine, dealer-site agents, a public
        pulse we call Live, the brand Co-Pilot when an OEM wants the market
        without a sandbox. Coriolis publishes those products. I am the
        operator. This site is moonlight. A one-woman wire. I will not invent
        a Live number for March and park it next to minus 11.9 as if I had a
        competing index. I will say this: a zero-hit search never becomes a
        same-store unit. It still happened. The print cannot see it. That is
        why I file at night.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-10">
      <p
        class="text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-sky-400 mb-3"
      >
        Three files. Do not staple them.
      </p>
      <ThreeNumbersTable />
    </div>

    <div
      class="mt-8 space-y-5 text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.7] text-zinc-300 font-serif"
    >
      <p>
        I will file again when the next Index hits Outdoor Wire. Until then
        the long comparison of the two factories — ops stack versus discovery
        stack — lives on GunSearchEngine, not here:{" "}
        <a href={LINKS.fortress} class={linkClass} {...ext}
          >RetailBI and Betsy, side by side</a
        >. If you run a floor and want the microphone on your own site, that
        is{" "}
        <a href={LINKS.gsa} class={linkClass} {...ext}>GunSearchAgent</a>, and
        it is a different product than this brief.
      </p>
      <p>That’s the file. Betsy. Night desk.</p>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-10">
      <LegalNote />
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
